(voxelf: the ELF writer of the Vox toolchain.

 It takes the assembled bytes of a program's sections and writes a flat,
 static, position-dependent x86-64 executable: no interpreter, no dynamic
 section and no relocations, the same shape ld gives a Vox program. Today
 the bytes come from nasm -f bin and a small text manifest says where each
 section lies in them; an assembler written in Vox will instead call
 'place the sections' between measuring its sections and encoding them, and
 hand the bytes to 'write the executable and count the errors'.

 Two surfaces, for two kinds of caller.

 A host that is not Vox passes numbers and file paths only:

   'link and keep the diagnostics' of <manifest> and <output> and <entry name>
       writes the executable; answers 0, or how many errors there were
   'lay out and keep the diagnostics' of <manifest> and <output>
       writes the manifest back with every section placed; answers the same
   'linker diagnostics' of <output>
       what went wrong in the last call that wrote to <output>, as text

 A Vox caller does the same work in process, and owns the buffer the
 diagnostics are collected in:

   'link and count the errors' with <manifest> and <output> and <entry name> and <buffer>
   'lay out and count the errors' with <manifest> and <output> and <buffer>
   'place the sections' with <sections> and <load base> and <buffer>
   'write the executable and count the errors' with <sections> and <image>
       and <entry address> and <load base> and <output> and <buffer>

 Nothing here prints and nothing exits. A failure is the number of errors
 handed back, one line of diagnostics for each error, and, for a Vox
 caller, the error flag raised. The manifest format is described in
 README.md beside this file.)

(This file holds voxelf's functions and has no Library line of its own:
 voxelf.vox gives them the library's name, and another library can see
 this file to take them in under its name instead.)

(--- Bytes ---------------------------------------------------------------)

(One byte onto the end of a buffer. Writing one past the end is what grows
 a dynamic buffer, so this needs no scratch of its own.)
To 'append the byte' with a number called 'the byte' and a buffer called output.
    Set byte {output's size add 1} of output to 'the byte'.

(A number written as width bytes, lowest byte first: the order every field
 of an x86-64 ELF file takes.)
To 'append a little-endian number' with a number called amount and a number called width and a buffer called output.
    a number called 'what is left' is amount.
    Repeat width times,
        'append the byte' with {'what is left' bit-and 255} and output,
        Set 'what is left' to 'what is left' bit-shift-right 8.

(Zero bytes until the buffer is as long as asked, which is how each section
 comes to start at its own file offset.)
To 'pad with zeros' with a buffer called output and a number called 'wanted size'.
    While output's size is less than 'wanted size',
        'append the byte' with 0 and output.

(The bytes of one section, copied out of the image it was assembled into.
 Positions in a buffer count from 1, and offsets in an image from 0.)
To 'copy the section bytes' with a buffer called image and a number called 'image offset' and a number called 'section size' and a buffer called output.
    For each position from 1 to 'section size',
        'append the byte' with {byte {'image offset' add position} of image} and output.

(--- Small answers --------------------------------------------------------)

(The first multiple of alignment at or after position. An alignment of 0
 or 1 asks for nothing.)
To 'the boundary at or after' with a number called position and a number called alignment.
    If alignment is less than 2 then, Return a number, position.
    a number called overhang is position modulo alignment.
    If overhang is 0 then, Return a number, position.
    Return a number, position add {alignment subtract overhang}.

To 'the larger of' with a number called 'an amount' and a number called 'another amount'.
    If 'an amount' is greater than 'another amount' then, Return a number, 'an amount'.
    Return a number, 'another amount'.

To 'is a power of two' of a number called candidate.
    If candidate is less than 1 then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, {candidate bit-and {candidate subtract 1}} is 0.

To 'is one of' with a text called candidate and a list called choices.
    For each choice in choices,
        if choice is candidate then, Return a boolean, true..
    Return a boolean, false.

(How many bytes a text takes, which is what a name costs in the string
 table. A text has no size of its own; the buffer made from it does.)
To 'the byte size of' with a text called phrase.
    a buffer called spelled is "{phrase}".
    Return a number, spelled's size.

(--- Diagnostics ----------------------------------------------------------)

(One error, as one line of diagnostics.)
To 'report' with a buffer called diagnostics and a text called complaint.
    append "{complaint}\n" to diagnostics.

(Each diagnostic is one line, so counting the newlines counts the errors.)
To 'the number of diagnostics in' with a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'lines found' is 0.
    For each position from 1 to diagnostics's size,
        if byte position of diagnostics is '\n' then, increment 'lines found'..
    Return a number, 'lines found'.

(Raising the error flag is something Vox gives an operation, not a
 statement, so this performs the one operation whose only job here is to
 fail: reading a number out of a text that is not one. The text is held in
 a variable because the compiler refuses to cast a literal it can see is
 not a number.)
To 'raise the error flag'.
    a text called 'words that are not a number' is "not a number".
    a number called 'the failed reading' is 'words that are not a number' as a number.

(How many errors were reported since the count stood at 'errors before',
 with the error flag raised when there were any. Every function that counts
 the errors for a Vox caller ends by returning this, so raising the flag is
 the last thing it does: the next operation that can fail would clear the
 flag again.)
To 'the errors since' with a number called 'errors before' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called errors is {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If errors is greater than 0 then, 'raise the error flag'.
    Return a number, errors.

(--- ELF numbers ----------------------------------------------------------)

(The sizes of the three kinds of header, which every x86-64 ELF file
 states in its own file header.)
To 'the file header size'. Return a number, 64.

To 'the program header size'. Return a number, 56.

To 'the section header size'. Return a number, 64.

(The loader maps a segment a page at a time, so a segment's file offset and
 its address must agree modulo a page, and no section may ask for more
 alignment than a page.)
To 'the page size'. Return a number, 4096.

(The most memory the sections of one program may need, 128 TiB: one half of
 the x86-64 address space. Holding every size and the total below it keeps
 every sum voxelf works out far below 2^63, so none can wrap round to a
 small number and pass a check.)
To 'the largest span'. Return a number, 0x800000000000.

(The two halves of the x86-64 address space where an address is canonical:
 the lower half, from 0 up to 2^47, and the upper half, from
 0xffff800000000000 up to 2^64, where a kernel such as Linux is linked, at
 0xffffffff80000000. The addresses between the halves are not canonical, so
 nothing can be mapped there, and a program lies wholly in one half or the
 other. Vox holds a number of the upper half as the number below zero with
 the same 64 bits, so the upper half is the numbers from -2^47 up to 0.
 With every size below 2^47, every sum of an address and a size stays
 between -2^47 and 2^48, far from wrapping round as Vox counts.)
To 'the top of the lower half'. Return a number, 0x800000000000.

To 'the bottom of the upper half'. Return a number, 0 subtract 0x800000000000.

To 'is in the lower half' of a number called address.
    If address is less than 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, address is less than 'the top of the lower half'.

To 'is in the upper half' of a number called address.
    If address is greater than or equal to 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, address is greater than or equal to 'the bottom of the upper half'.

(Linux maps nothing below 64 KiB unless told to, its vm.mmap_min_addr
 setting, so a program placed lower could not be started. The upper half
 has no such floor.)
To 'the lowest load base'. Return a number, 0x10000.

(The most voxelf reads of a manifest and of an image. A manifest is a line
 or two for each section and symbol, and an image is a program's bytes; a
 file larger than these is refused before it can fill the memory, as
 /dev/zero, which never ends, otherwise would.)
To 'the largest manifest'. Return a number, 16777216.

To 'the largest image'. Return a number, 268435456.

(The largest number Vox holds, 2^63 - 1.)
To 'the largest number'. Return a number, 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.

To 'the loadable segment type'. Return a number, 1.

(PT_GNU_STACK: a segment that holds nothing and only says what the stack
 may do. voxelf always writes one, read and write but not execute, which
 is what ld writes for an object that carries a .note.GNU-stack section, as
 every program the compiler writes does. It puts in the file that the stack
 is not executable, where a program without the header gets whatever the
 kernel defaults to, and older kernels map its stack executable.)
To 'the stack segment type'. Return a number, 0x6474E551.

(A segment's permissions are bits: 4 to read, 2 to write, 1 to execute.)
To 'read and execute'. Return a number, 5.

To 'read only'. Return a number, 4.

To 'read and write'. Return a number, 6.

(--- ELF structures -------------------------------------------------------)

(The ELF header of an x86-64 executable. A section header count of 0 says
 there is no section header table; otherwise the table ends with the names
 section, .shstrtab, so its index is the last one.)
To 'append the elf header' with a buffer called output and a number called 'entry address' and a number called 'program header count' and a number called 'section header offset' and a number called 'section header count'.
    a number called 'names section index' is 0.
    If 'section header count' is greater than 0 then, Set 'names section index' to 'section header count' subtract 1.
    (\x7fELF, read as one little-endian word)
    a number called 'the magic number' is 0x464C457F.
    a number called 'the 64-bit class' is 2.
    a number called 'little-endian' is 1.
    a number called 'the current version' is 1.
    a number called 'an executable' is 2.
    a number called 'the x86-64 machine' is 62.
    a number called 'no processor flags' is 0.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the magic number' and 4 and output.
    'append the byte' with 'the 64-bit class' and output.
    'append the byte' with 'little-endian' and output.
    'append the byte' with 'the current version' and output.
    (The System V ABI, its version 0, and seven bytes of padding)
    'append a little-endian number' with 0 and 9 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'an executable' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the x86-64 machine' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the current version' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'entry address' and 8 and output.
    (The program headers start where this header ends)
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the file header size' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'section header offset' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'no processor flags' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the file header size' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the program header size' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'program header count' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'the section header size' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'section header count' and 2 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'names section index' and 2 and output.

(One program header. A static executable loads at the addresses it names,
 so the physical address repeats the virtual one, as ld writes it.)
To 'append a program header' with a buffer called output and a number called 'segment type' and a number called 'segment permissions' and a number called 'file offset' and a number called address and a number called 'file size' and a number called 'memory size' and a number called alignment.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'segment type' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'segment permissions' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'file offset' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with address and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with address and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'file size' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'memory size' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with alignment and 8 and output.

(One section header. Nothing voxelf writes links one section to another or
 holds fixed-size entries, so those three fields are zero.)
To 'append a section header' with a buffer called output and a number called 'name offset' and a number called 'section type' and a number called 'section flags' and a number called address and a number called 'file offset' and a number called 'section size' and a number called alignment.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'name offset' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'section type' and 4 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'section flags' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with address and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'file offset' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 'section size' and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 0 and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with alignment and 8 and output.
    'append a little-endian number' with 0 and 8 and output.

(--- Sections -------------------------------------------------------------)

(The four kinds of section, in the order they are laid out: code, read-only
 data, initialised data, and zero-filled data that takes no room in the
 file. Each kind has its segment: text alone, read-only data alone, and
 initialised and zero-filled data together, the way ld groups them.)
To 'is a known kind' of a text called kind.
    Return a boolean, 'is one of' with kind and ["text", "rodata", "data", "bss"].

(The ELF section type: zero-filled data has no bytes in the file, and every
 other kind is plain program bytes.)
To 'the section type for' of a text called kind.
    If kind is "bss" then, Return a number, 8.
    Return a number, 1.

(The ELF section flags: 2 to occupy memory, 1 to be writable, 4 to be
 executable.)
To 'the section flags for' of a text called kind.
    If kind is "text" then, Return a number, 6.
    If kind is "rodata" then, Return a number, 2.
    Return a number, 3.

To 'has the key' with a map called section and a text called key.
    a list called 'keys it has' is section's keys.
    Return a boolean, 'is one of' with key and 'keys it has'.

To 'the number of sections of kind' with a list called sections and a text called kind.
    a number called 'sections found' is 0.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to sections's length,
        a map called section is element position of sections,
        a text called 'its kind' is section's "kind",
        if 'its kind' is kind then, increment 'sections found'..
    Return a number, 'sections found'.

(Every executable has a text segment and a stack header. Read-only data and
 writable data each add a segment of their own when there is any.)
To 'the program header count for' with a list called sections.
    a number called headers is 2.
    a number called 'read-only sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "rodata".
    a number called 'data sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "data".
    a number called 'zeroed sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "bss".
    If 'read-only sections' is greater than 0 then, increment headers.
    If {'data sections' add 'zeroed sections'} is greater than 0 then, increment headers.
    Return a number, headers.

(Everything placement needs to be true of the sections, checked before any
 of them is placed, so a bad manifest gets every complaint in one run. The
 sizes are added up only while the total stays within the largest span, so
 the total itself can never wrap round. A load base must lie in one of the
 two halves of the address space; a base between them is refused here, and
 'place the sections' refuses memory that runs on out of its half.)
To 'check the sections' with a list called sections and a number called 'load base' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a list called 'names seen' is [].
    a number called 'total size' is 0.
    a boolean called 'the total is too large' is false.
    a number called 'half of the address space' is 'the largest span'.
    a number called 'lowest base' is 'the lowest load base'.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to sections's length,
        a map called section is element position of sections,
        a text called 'section name' is section's "name",
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        a number called alignment is section's "alignment",
        if 'is one of' with 'section name' and 'names seen' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} is described more than once; describe each section once".
        append 'section name' to 'names seen',
        if not 'is a known kind' of kind then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name}: '{kind}' is not a kind of section; a section is text, rodata, data or bss".
        if 'section size' is less than 0 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} has no size".
        if 'section size' is greater than 'half of the address space' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name}: a size of {section size} bytes is more than the {half of the address space} bytes of one half of the address space".
        if 'section size' is greater than 0 and 'section size' is less than or equal to 'half of the address space' and not 'the total is too large' then,
            Set 'total size' to 'total size' add 'section size',
            if 'total size' is greater than 'half of the address space' then, Set 'the total is too large' to true..
        if not 'is a power of two' of alignment then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name}: an alignment of {alignment} is not a power of two".
        if alignment is greater than 'the page size' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name}: an alignment of {alignment} is more than a page, which the loader cannot honour"..
    If 'the total is too large' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the sections need more than the {half of the address space} bytes of one half of the address space".
    a number called 'text sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "text".
    If 'text sections' is 0 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "there is no text section, so the executable would have nothing to run".
    If 'load base' bit-and {'the page size' subtract 1} is not 0 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the load base {load base:x} does not start a page".
    a boolean called 'it is in the lower half' is 'is in the lower half' of 'load base'.
    a boolean called 'it is in the upper half' is 'is in the upper half' of 'load base'.
    If 'it is in the lower half' and 'load base' is less than 'the lowest load base' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the load base {load base:x} is below {lowest base:x}, where Linux maps nothing by default, so the program could not be started".
    a number called 'top of the lower half' is 'the top of the lower half'.
    a number called 'bottom of the upper half' is 'the bottom of the upper half'.
    If not 'it is in the lower half' and not 'it is in the upper half' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the load base {load base:x} lies between {top of the lower half:x} and {bottom of the upper half:x}, between the two halves of the address space, where no address is canonical and nothing can be mapped".

(--- Placement ------------------------------------------------------------)

(Where every section goes, before a byte of it is written: the address its
 code is assembled for and the file offset its bytes land at. An assembler
 calls this between measuring its sections and encoding them, and the
 writer calls it again to check it gets the same answer.

 Each section is a map holding "name", "kind", "size" and "alignment". What
 comes back is a new list of the same sections in layout order, each with
 "address" and "file offset" added. It is a new list because layout order
 groups the sections by kind, which need not be the order the caller gave,
 and the caller's list is left as it was. Each section in it is the copy
 'place the sections of kind' makes, which needs the naming-copy fix (#128)
 and the fix for #156, unreleased as of 0.4.16; see docs/bugs/unreleased.md.

 The file starts with the headers, inside the text segment, the way ld lays
 out a static executable, and every section starts at the next multiple of
 its own alignment. The segment after the code starts on a fresh page of
 the file, as ld keeps code apart, so no page of the file holds both code
 and data and no data is mapped executable. After that the file is packed:
 the writable data follows the read-only data at its own alignment, and
 starts in memory on the first page after the read-only data ends, at the
 same place in that page as in its page of the file, unless a fresh page
 would save a page of memory, when it starts on one, as ld chooses (see 'a
 fresh page saves memory for'). So a file offset and its address always
 agree modulo the page size, which is what the loader requires, and no two
 segments share a page of memory, so each keeps its own permissions.

 The cursors count from the start of the file and from the load base, so
 every number placement works with is small and at least zero. Every
 segment must lie wholly in the half of the address space its load base is
 in: a program whose memory would run on from the lower half into the
 addresses between the halves, or past the top of the upper half, where it
 would wrap round to 0, is refused.)
To 'place the sections' with a list called sections and a number called 'load base' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a list called placed is [].
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    'check the sections' with sections and 'load base' and diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors found' is 'the errors since' with 'errors before' and diagnostics.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return a list, placed.
    a number called 'program header count' is 'the program header count for' with sections.
    a number called 'header size' is 'the file header size' add {'program header count' multiply 'the program header size'}.
    a map called cursors is {"file": 0, "memory": 0}.
    Set cursors's "file" to 'header size'.
    Set cursors's "memory" to 'header size'.
    'place the sections of kind' with "text" and sections and placed and cursors and 'load base'.
    a number called 'read-only sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "rodata".
    If 'read-only sections' is greater than 0 then, 'turn to a fresh page' with cursors.
    'place the sections of kind' with "rodata" and sections and placed and cursors and 'load base'.
    a number called 'data sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "data".
    a number called 'zeroed sections' is 'the number of sections of kind' with sections and "bss".
    a number called 'writable sections' is 'data sections' add 'zeroed sections'.
    a boolean called 'it follows read-only data' is false.
    If 'writable sections' is greater than 0 and 'read-only sections' is greater than 0 then, Set 'it follows read-only data' to true.
    a boolean called 'a fresh page saves memory' is false.
    If 'it follows read-only data' then, Set 'a fresh page saves memory' to 'a fresh page saves memory for' with sections and cursors.
    If 'writable sections' is greater than 0 and 'read-only sections' is 0 then, 'turn to a fresh page' with cursors.
    If 'it follows read-only data' and 'a fresh page saves memory' then, 'turn to a fresh page' with cursors.
    If 'it follows read-only data' and not 'a fresh page saves memory' then, 'turn to the next page in memory' with cursors.
    'place the sections of kind' with "data" and sections and placed and cursors and 'load base'.
    'place the sections of kind' with "bss" and sections and placed and cursors and 'load base'.
    a number called 'memory needed' is cursors's "memory".
    If 'memory needed' is greater than 'the largest span' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the sections need {memory needed} bytes of memory once aligned, more than one half of the address space".
    (The segments run from the load base to the end of the last one, so
     they lie in the base's half when that end does. ld pads the last
     segment's memory to 8 bytes and the loader maps whole pages, and both
     ends of each half are multiples of a page, so neither padding moves an
     end that is inside the half outside it.)
    a number called 'top of the lower half' is 'the top of the lower half'.
    If {'is in the lower half' of 'load base'} and 'memory needed' is greater than {'top of the lower half' subtract 'load base'} then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the sections need {memory needed} bytes of memory, which from the load base {load base:x} would run past {top of the lower half:x}, the top of the lower half of the address space, into the addresses between the halves, where no address is canonical".
    (The distance from an address of the upper half to 2^64 is the number
     below zero that Vox holds it as, negated)
    If {'is in the upper half' of 'load base'} and 'memory needed' is greater than {0 subtract 'load base'} then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the sections need {memory needed} bytes of memory, which from the load base {load base:x} would run past the top of the address space and wrap round to 0".
    Set 'errors found' to 'the errors since' with 'errors before' and diagnostics.
    a list called 'nothing placed' is [].
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return a list, 'nothing placed'.
    Return a list, placed.

(Where ld starts the writable segment when read-only data comes before it.
 Its linker script places it with DATA_SEGMENT_ALIGN, which first tries the
 place this file uses by default, after the last page of the read-only
 data at the same place in the page as the file has got to, costing no
 bytes of file. If the segment would then start part way into one page and
 end part way into another, and the two parts would fit together in one
 page, starting on a fresh page needs one page less of memory, for at most
 a page of padding in the file, and ld takes that instead. The end ld
 measures is DATA_SEGMENT_END, after the zero-filled data, padded to 8
 bytes.

 The test is made on copies, so the cursors are left as they were: a map
 named from another is a copy of its own (LANGUAGE.md, Naming copies). This
 needs the naming-copy fix (#128), unreleased as of 0.4.16; see
 docs/bugs/unreleased.md.)
To 'a fresh page saves memory for' with a list called sections and a map called cursors.
    a map called 'trial cursors' is cursors.
    a list called 'trial placement' is [].
    'turn to the next page in memory' with 'trial cursors'.
    a number called 'segment start' is 'trial cursors''s "memory".
    (Only the cursors are read afterwards, so the trial places from a base of 0)
    'place the sections of kind' with "data" and sections and 'trial placement' and 'trial cursors' and 0.
    'place the sections of kind' with "bss" and sections and 'trial placement' and 'trial cursors' and 0.
    a number called 'segment end' is 'the boundary at or after' with {'trial cursors''s "memory"} and 8.
    a number called 'part of its first page' is {'the page size' subtract {'segment start' modulo 'the page size'}} modulo 'the page size'.
    a number called 'part of its last page' is 'segment end' modulo 'the page size'.
    If 'part of its first page' is 0 or 'part of its last page' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    If {'segment start' divide 'the page size'} is {'segment end' divide 'the page size'} then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, {'part of its first page' add 'part of its last page'} is less than or equal to 'the page size'.

(The segment after the code starts on the next page boundary of the file,
 and on the next one in memory, so the pages of the file that hold code
 hold nothing else.)
To 'turn to a fresh page' with a map called cursors.
    a number called 'file cursor' is cursors's "file".
    a number called 'memory cursor' is cursors's "memory".
    Set cursors's "file" to 'the boundary at or after' with 'file cursor' and 'the page size'.
    Set cursors's "memory" to 'the boundary at or after' with 'memory cursor' and 'the page size'.

(A segment after the read-only data stays where the file has got to, and
 moves in memory to the first page after the read-only data ends, at the
 same place in that page as the file cursor is in its page. The two
 cursors still agree modulo the page size, and the new segment shares no
 page of memory with the one before it.)
To 'turn to the next page in memory' with a map called cursors.
    a number called 'file cursor' is cursors's "file".
    a number called 'memory cursor' is cursors's "memory".
    a number called 'page start' is 'the boundary at or after' with 'memory cursor' and 'the page size'.
    Set cursors's "memory" to 'page start' add {'file cursor' modulo 'the page size'}.

(Places every section of one kind, in the order the caller listed them. The
 file cursor counts bytes from the start of the file and the memory cursor
 from the load base. The two agree modulo the page size and no alignment is
 larger than a page, so moving the file cursor to a section's alignment
 moves the memory cursor the same distance to the same alignment.
 Zero-filled sections follow the others in memory and take no room in the
 file, so they move only the memory cursor, and each is given the file
 offset where the file's bytes stopped, as ld gives it.

 Each placed section is a copy of the caller's, so the caller's sections
 are left as they were (LANGUAGE.md, Naming copies). The copy needs the
 naming-copy fix (#128), and appending a map read out of a list parameter
 into a local needs the fix for #156, both unreleased as of 0.4.16; see
 docs/bugs/unreleased.md. The two kinds of section are an If and an
 Otherwise, and the periods that close the Otherwise, the If around it and
 the loop need the Otherwise-period fix (#160), unreleased as of 0.4.16; see
 docs/bugs/unreleased.md.)
To 'place the sections of kind' with a text called kind and a list called sections and a list called placed and a map called cursors and a number called 'load base'.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to sections's length,
        a map called section is element position of sections,
        a text called 'its kind' is section's "kind",
        if 'its kind' is kind then,
            a map called 'placed section' is section,
            a number called alignment is section's "alignment",
            a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
            a number called 'file cursor' is cursors's "file",
            a number called 'memory cursor' is cursors's "memory",
            if kind is "bss" then,
                a number called 'memory offset' is 'the boundary at or after' with 'memory cursor' and alignment,
                Set 'placed section''s "address" to 'load base' add 'memory offset',
                Set 'placed section''s "file offset" to 'file cursor',
                Set cursors's "memory" to 'memory offset' add 'section size'.
            Otherwise,
                a number called 'file offset' is 'the boundary at or after' with 'file cursor' and alignment,
                a number called 'memory offset' is 'memory cursor' add {'file offset' subtract 'file cursor'},
                Set 'placed section''s "address" to 'load base' add 'memory offset',
                Set 'placed section''s "file offset" to 'file offset',
                Set cursors's "file" to 'file offset' add 'section size',
                Set cursors's "memory" to 'memory offset' add 'section size'.
            append 'placed section' to placed..

(--- Segments -------------------------------------------------------------)

(The loadable segments, read back off the placed sections. Each covers its
 sections from the first byte to the last, in the file and in memory, and
 starts at its first section's file offset and address; the text segment
 also covers the headers, which open the file inside it.)
To 'the segments of' with a list called placed and a number called 'load base' and a number called 'header size'.
    a list called segments is [].
    'add the segment for' with placed and ["text"] and 'read and execute' and 'load base' and true and 'header size' and segments.
    'add the segment for' with placed and ["rodata"] and 'read only' and 'load base' and false and 0 and segments.
    'add the segment for' with placed and ["data", "bss"] and 'read and write' and 'load base' and false and 0 and segments.
    Return a list, segments.

(One segment for every placed section whose kind is among kinds. A group
 with no sections has no segment, except the one that holds the headers:
 it starts at the very beginning of the file, whatever else it holds.

 Memory is counted from the load base, as placement counts it, so every
 number compared here is small and at least zero, wherever the base is.)
To 'add the segment for' with a list called placed and a list called kinds and a number called 'segment permissions' and a number called 'load base' and a boolean called 'holds the headers' and a number called 'header size' and a list called segments.
    a number called 'sections in it' is 0.
    a boolean called 'it holds zero-filled data' is false.
    a number called 'first offset' is 0.
    a number called 'segment start' is 0.
    a number called 'file end' is 0.
    a number called 'memory end' is 0.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        if 'is one of' with kind and kinds then,
            a number called 'file offset' is section's "file offset",
            a number called address is section's "address",
            a number called 'memory offset' is address subtract 'load base',
            a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
            if 'sections in it' is 0 then, Set 'first offset' to 'file offset', Set 'segment start' to 'memory offset', Set 'file end' to 'file offset', Set 'memory end' to 'memory offset'.
            (A segment that opens with zero-filled data has no bytes in the
             file, and its file offset only has to agree with its address
             modulo the page size. ld gives it the address's place in its
             page, or a whole page when that place is 0.)
            if 'sections in it' is 0 and kind is "bss" then,
                Set 'first offset' to 'memory offset' modulo 'the page size',
                if 'first offset' is 0 then, Set 'first offset' to 'the page size'.
                Set 'file end' to 'first offset'.
            if kind is "bss" then, Set 'it holds zero-filled data' to true.
            if kind is not "bss" then, Set 'file end' to 'the larger of' with 'file end' and {'file offset' add 'section size'}.
            Set 'memory end' to 'the larger of' with 'memory end' and {'memory offset' add 'section size'},
            increment 'sections in it'..
    If not 'holds the headers' and 'sections in it' is 0 then, Return.
    If 'holds the headers' then,
        Set 'first offset' to 0,
        Set 'segment start' to 0,
        Set 'file end' to 'the larger of' with 'file end' and 'header size',
        Set 'memory end' to 'the larger of' with 'memory end' and 'file end'.
    (ld's linker script pads .bss to a multiple of 8 bytes, with ALIGN(. != 0
     ? 64 / 8 : 1) inside it, and the segment ends where .bss ends)
    If 'it holds zero-filled data' then, Set 'memory end' to 'the boundary at or after' with 'memory end' and 8.
    a number called 'file size' is 'file end' subtract 'first offset'.
    a number called 'memory size' is 'memory end' subtract 'segment start'.
    a number called 'segment address' is 'load base' add 'segment start'.
    a map called segment is {"permissions": 'segment permissions', "file offset": 'first offset', "address": 'segment address', "file size": 'file size', "memory size": 'memory size'}.
    append segment to segments.

(--- Writing --------------------------------------------------------------)

(Every check that has to pass before a byte is written. Every section must
 say, as "assembled address", the address it was assembled for, and is
 refused if placement puts it anywhere else: the image's code and data
 point at the places they were assembled for, and voxelf applies no
 relocations to move them. A section with bytes needs them to lie inside
 the image, and the entry point has to lie inside the code.

 The image bounds are compared without adding the offset to the size, so
 an offset near 2^63 cannot wrap round to a small number and pass. The
 entry point is measured as its distance into each text section, which is
 below the section's size only when it lies inside, wherever in the
 address space the section is.)
To 'check the placed sections' with a list called placed and a buffer called image and a number called 'entry address' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a boolean called 'the entry is in the code' is false.
    a number called 'image size' is image's size.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called 'section name' is section's "name",
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called address is section's "address",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        a boolean called 'it names its address' is 'has the key' with section and "assembled address",
        a boolean called 'it names its bytes' is 'has the key' with section and "image offset",
        if not 'it names its address' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} does not say the address it was assembled for, so nothing shows that its addresses are the ones voxelf lays out; give its address, {address:x}, if that is the one it was assembled for".
        if 'it names its address' then,
            a number called 'assembled address' is section's "assembled address",
            if 'assembled address' is not address then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} was assembled for {assembled address:x} but is placed at {address:x}; assemble it for the addresses voxelf lays out"..
        if kind is not "bss" and 'section size' is greater than 0 then,
            if not 'it names its bytes' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} does not say where its bytes start in the image".
            if 'it names its bytes' then,
                a number called 'image offset' is section's "image offset",
                if 'image offset' is less than 0 or 'image offset' is greater than 'image size' or 'section size' is greater than {'image size' subtract 'image offset'} then, 'report' with diagnostics and "section {section name} runs for {section size} bytes from offset {image offset} in the image, which holds {image size} bytes"...
        a number called 'distance into it' is 'entry address' subtract address,
        if kind is "text" and 'distance into it' is greater than or equal to 0 and 'distance into it' is less than 'section size' then, Set 'the entry is in the code' to true..
    If not 'the entry is in the code' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "the entry point {entry address:x} is not inside a text section".

(The names of the sections, each ending in a zero byte, after the empty name
 every string table starts with, and last the table's own name.)
To 'the section names for' with a list called placed.
    Create a buffer called names.
    'append the byte' with 0 and names.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called 'section name' is section's "name",
        append "{section name}" to names,
        'append the byte' with 0 and names.
    append ".shstrtab" to names.
    'append the byte' with 0 and names.
    Return a buffer, names.

(The whole file: the ELF header, the program headers, every section's bytes
 at its file offset, then the section names and the section header table,
 so readelf -S and objdump -d can read the result.)
To 'the executable for' with a list called placed and a buffer called image and a number called 'entry address' and a number called 'load base'.
    a number called 'program header count' is 'the program header count for' with placed.
    a number called 'header size' is 'the file header size' add {'program header count' multiply 'the program header size'}.
    a list called segments is 'the segments of' with placed and 'load base' and 'header size'.
    a number called 'end of the bytes' is 'header size'.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called 'file offset' is section's "file offset",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        if kind is not "bss" then, Set 'end of the bytes' to 'the larger of' with 'end of the bytes' and {'file offset' add 'section size'}..
    a buffer called names is 'the section names for' with placed.
    a number called 'section header offset' is 'the boundary at or after' with {'end of the bytes' add names's size} and 8.
    a number called 'section header count' is placed's length add 2.
    Create a buffer called executable.
    'append the elf header' with executable and 'entry address' and 'program header count' and 'section header offset' and 'section header count'.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to segments's length,
        a map called segment is element position of segments,
        a number called 'segment permissions' is segment's "permissions",
        a number called 'file offset' is segment's "file offset",
        a number called address is segment's "address",
        a number called 'file size' is segment's "file size",
        a number called 'memory size' is segment's "memory size",
        'append a program header' with executable and 'the loadable segment type' and 'segment permissions' and 'file offset' and address and 'file size' and 'memory size' and 'the page size'.
    (The stack is never executable, which is what the .note.GNU-stack section
     in the compiler's output asks for. Its alignment of 16 is the one ld
     writes.)
    'append a program header' with executable and 'the stack segment type' and 'read and write' and 0 and 0 and 0 and 0 and 16.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called 'file offset' is section's "file offset",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        if kind is not "bss" and 'section size' is greater than 0 then,
            a number called 'image offset' is section's "image offset",
            'pad with zeros' with executable and 'file offset',
            'copy the section bytes' with image and 'image offset' and 'section size' and executable..
    'pad with zeros' with executable and 'end of the bytes'.
    append names to executable.
    'pad with zeros' with executable and 'section header offset'.
    (Section 0 is the empty section every table starts with)
    'pad with zeros' with executable and {'section header offset' add 'the section header size'}.
    a number called 'name offset' is 1.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called 'section name' is section's "name",
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called address is section's "address",
        a number called 'file offset' is section's "file offset",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        a number called alignment is section's "alignment",
        a number called 'section type' is 'the section type for' of kind,
        a number called 'section flags' is 'the section flags for' of kind,
        'append a section header' with executable and 'name offset' and 'section type' and 'section flags' and address and 'file offset' and 'section size' and alignment,
        Set 'name offset' to 'name offset' add {'the byte size of' with 'section name'} add 1.
    (The names section is a string table, type 3. It takes no memory, so it
     has no address and no flags.)
    'append a section header' with executable and 'name offset' and 3 and 0 and 0 and 'end of the bytes' and names's size and 1.
    Return a buffer, executable.

(The whole of a buffer into a file, created or emptied first. Vox opens a
 file for writing with mode 0644 and has no statement that changes a file's
 mode, so the executable is written without its execute bits; the caller
 sets them.)
To 'write the bytes' with a buffer called content and a text called path and a buffer called diagnostics.
    open a file for writing called destination at path.
    On error 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: cannot be opened for writing", Return.
    Write content to destination.
    On error 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: could not be written in full".
    Close destination.

(The writer for a Vox caller. The sections are maps as 'place the sections'
 takes them, each also holding "image offset", where its bytes start in the
 image, counting from 0, and "assembled address", the address it was
 assembled for. Answers 0, or how many errors there were; nothing is
 written unless there were none.)
To 'write the executable and count the errors' with a list called sections and a buffer called image and a number called 'entry address' and a number called 'load base' and a text called 'output path' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    'write the executable' with sections and image and 'entry address' and 'load base' and 'output path' and diagnostics.
    Return a number, 'the errors since' with 'errors before' and diagnostics.

(The work itself, which stops at the first stage that reports an error and
 leaves the counting to its caller.)
To 'write the executable' with a list called sections and a buffer called image and a number called 'entry address' and a number called 'load base' and a text called 'output path' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    a list called placed is 'place the sections' with sections and 'load base' and diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors found' is {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    'check the placed sections' with placed and image and 'entry address' and diagnostics.
    Set 'errors found' to {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    a buffer called executable is 'the executable for' with placed and image and 'entry address' and 'load base'.
    'write the bytes' with executable and 'output path' and diagnostics.

(--- Reading files --------------------------------------------------------)

(The whole of a file, read 64 KiB at a time until a read comes back empty,
 or nothing, with the complaint reported, once it has given more than
 'size limit' bytes. The pieces are read into a buffer of fixed size,
 because one read into a dynamic buffer takes everything the file has, and
 a file such as /dev/zero never runs out.)
To 'the contents of' with a text called path and a number called 'size limit' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    Create a buffer called whole.
    a buffer called piece is 65536 bytes in size.
    open a file for reading called source at path.
    Read from source into piece.
    While piece is not empty,
        append piece to whole,
        if whole's size is greater than 'size limit' then,
            'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is more than {size limit} bytes long, more than voxelf reads",
            Close source,
            clear whole,
            Return a buffer, whole.
        Read from source into piece.
    Close source.
    Return a buffer, whole.

(A path in a manifest is read from the manifest's own directory unless it
 starts at the root, so a manifest and its image can move together.)
To 'the path beside' with a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'named path'.
    a buffer called 'named spelling' is "{named path}".
    If byte 1 of 'named spelling' is '/' then, Return a text, 'named path'.
    a buffer called 'manifest spelling' is "{manifest path}".
    a number called 'last slash' is 0.
    For each position from 1 to 'manifest spelling''s size,
        if byte position of 'manifest spelling' is '/' then, Set 'last slash' to position..
    If 'last slash' is 0 then, Return a text, 'named path'.
    Create a buffer called directory.
    For each position from 1 to 'last slash',
        'append the byte' with {byte position of 'manifest spelling'} and directory.
    a text called 'joined path' is "{directory}{named path}".
    Return a text, 'joined path'.

(--- ELF files ------------------------------------------------------------)

To 'is an elf file' with a buffer called content.
    If content's size is less than 20 then, Return a boolean, false.
    If byte 1 of content is not 0x7F then, Return a boolean, false.
    If byte 2 of content is not 'E' then, Return a boolean, false.
    If byte 3 of content is not 'L' then, Return a boolean, false.
    If byte 4 of content is not 'F' then, Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, true.

(voxelf does not read ELF files yet: a program reaches it as a raw image
 and a manifest. Each kind of ELF file is refused with what it is and what
 voxelf takes instead. The class is byte 5, the type the two bytes at
 offset 16, and the machine the two at offset 18.)
To 'refuse the elf file' with a buffer called content and a text called path and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'elf class' is byte 5 of content.
    a number called 'file type' is {byte 17 of content} add {{byte 18 of content} multiply 256}.
    a number called machine is {byte 19 of content} add {{byte 20 of content} multiply 256}.
    If 'elf class' is 1 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is a 32-bit ELF file; voxelf does not read ELF files yet, and writes only 64-bit executables so far", Return.
    If machine is not 62 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is built for ELF machine {machine}, not x86-64 (62); voxelf does not read ELF files yet, and writes only x86-64 executables so far", Return.
    If 'file type' is 1 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is a relocatable object; voxelf does not read relocatable objects or apply relocations yet, so assemble the program with nasm -f bin and describe the image with a manifest", Return.
    If 'file type' is 3 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is a shared object or a position-independent executable; voxelf does not link shared objects or write position-independent executables yet, only static executables", Return.
    If 'file type' is 2 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is already a linked executable", Return.
    'report' with diagnostics and "{path}: is an ELF file of type {file type}, which voxelf does not read".

(--- The manifest ---------------------------------------------------------)

(The words of one manifest line: runs of anything but spaces, tabs and
 carriage returns, so a manifest written with Windows line endings reads
 as the same words.)
To 'the words of' with a buffer called 'manifest line'.
    a list called words is [].
    Create a buffer called word.
    For each position from 1 to 'manifest line''s size,
        a number called 'the byte' is byte position of 'manifest line',
        a boolean called 'it is a space' is false,
        if 'the byte' is ' ' then, Set 'it is a space' to true.
        if 'the byte' is '\t' then, Set 'it is a space' to true.
        (13 is a carriage return, which has no escape of its own, LANGUAGE.md, Escape Sequences)
        if 'the byte' is 13 then, Set 'it is a space' to true.
        if not 'it is a space' then, 'append the byte' with 'the byte' and word.
        if 'it is a space' and word's size is greater than 0 then, append "{word}" to words, clear word..
    If word's size is greater than 0 then, append "{word}" to words.
    Return a list, words.

(The value of one digit, up to f in either case, or -1 when the byte is
 not a digit.)
To 'the value of the digit' with a number called 'the byte'.
    If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to '0' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to '9' then, Return a number, 'the byte' subtract '0'.
    If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'a' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'f' then, Return a number, 'the byte' subtract 'a' add 10.
    If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'A' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'F' then, Return a number, 'the byte' subtract 'A' add 10.
    Return a number, -1.

(The 64 bits a manifest word spells, in decimal, or in hexadecimal after 0x
 or 0X. A word that is not a number is reported, and reads as 0.

 Both are read digit by digit rather than cast. A cast keeps the whole part
 of a fraction, so "12.9" would read as 12 (LANGUAGE.md, Casting Rules), and
 it refuses anything at or above 2^63, which an address can be. Up to sixteen hexadecimal digits give all 64 bits, so an
 address in the upper half reads as the number below zero that has the same
 bits, which is how Vox holds it. A decimal word must stay below 2^63.)
To 'the bits spelled by' with a text called word and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a buffer called spelling is "{word}".
    a number called base is 10.
    a number called 'first digit' is 1.
    If spelling's size is greater than 2 then,
        if byte 1 of spelling is '0' and byte 2 of spelling is 'x' then, Set base to 16, Set 'first digit' to 3.
        if byte 1 of spelling is '0' and byte 2 of spelling is 'X' then, Set base to 16, Set 'first digit' to 3..
    a number called amount is 0.
    a number called 'digits that count' is 0.
    a boolean called 'every byte is a digit' is true.
    a boolean called 'it is too large' is false.
    If spelling's size is less than 1 then, Set 'every byte is a digit' to false.
    For each position from 'first digit' to spelling's size,
        a number called digit is 'the value of the digit' with {byte position of spelling},
        if digit is less than 0 or digit is greater than or equal to base then, Set 'every byte is a digit' to false.
        if 'every byte is a digit' and base is 16 then,
            if 'digits that count' is greater than 0 or digit is greater than 0 then, increment 'digits that count'.
            Set amount to {amount bit-shift-left 4} bit-or digit.
        if 'every byte is a digit' and base is 10 then,
            if amount is greater than {'the largest number' subtract digit} divide 10 then, Set 'it is too large' to true.
            if not 'it is too large' then, Set amount to {amount multiply 10} add digit...
    If not 'every byte is a digit' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{word}' is not a number", Return a number, 0.
    If 'digits that count' is greater than 16 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: {word} has more than 64 bits", Return a number, 0.
    If 'it is too large' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: {word} is 2^63 or more, which voxelf reads only in hexadecimal", Return a number, 0.
    Return a number, amount.

(A size, an alignment or an offset: a number from 0 up to 2^63 - 1. -1 is
 the answer when the word is not one, with the complaint reported.)
To 'the number in' with a text called word and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a buffer called spelling is "{word}".
    If byte 1 of spelling is '-' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: {word} is negative", Return a number, -1.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    a number called amount is 'the bits spelled by' with word and location and diagnostics.
    If {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} is greater than 'errors before' then, Return a number, -1.
    If amount is less than 0 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: {word} is 2^63 or more, more than any size, alignment or offset can be", Return a number, -1.
    Return a number, amount.

(An address: any 64 bits, the upper half of the address space included.)
To 'the address in' with a text called word and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    Return a number, 'the bits spelled by' with word and location and diagnostics.

(Reads a whole manifest, a line at a time. What the lines say is gathered
 into settings ("base" and "base location", the line that gave it, and
 "image" and "image location", the line that named it), sections, and
 symbols, a map from each symbol's name to its address.

 The two cases of a byte are an If and an Otherwise, and the two periods
 after the Otherwise, which close it and the loop, need the Otherwise-period
 fix (#160), unreleased as of 0.4.16; see docs/bugs/unreleased.md.)
To 'read the manifest' with a buffer called content and a text called 'manifest path' and a map called settings and a list called sections and a map called symbols and a buffer called diagnostics.
    Create a buffer called 'manifest line'.
    a number called 'line number' is 1.
    For each position from 1 to content's size,
        a number called 'the byte' is byte position of content,
        if 'the byte' is not '\n' then, 'append the byte' with 'the byte' and 'manifest line'.
        Otherwise,
            'read the manifest line' with 'manifest line' and "{manifest path}:{line number}" and settings and sections and symbols and diagnostics,
            clear 'manifest line',
            increment 'line number'..
    If 'manifest line''s size is greater than 0 then, 'read the manifest line' with 'manifest line' and "{manifest path}:{line number}" and settings and sections and symbols and diagnostics.

(One line. A blank line, and a line whose first word starts with #, say
 nothing. Every other line starts with the word that says what it is, and
 the things voxelf does not do are refused by name rather than ignored.)
To 'read the manifest line' with a buffer called 'manifest line' and a text called location and a map called settings and a list called sections and a map called symbols and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a list called words is 'the words of' with 'manifest line'.
    If words's length is 0 then, Return.
    a text called keyword is element 1 of words.
    a buffer called 'keyword spelling' is "{keyword}".
    If byte 1 of 'keyword spelling' is '#' then, Return.
    If keyword is "section" then, 'read a section line' with words and location and sections and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "symbol" then, 'read a symbol line' with words and location and symbols and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "image" then, 'read an image line' with words and location and settings and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "base" then, 'read a base line' with words and location and settings and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "machine" then, 'read a machine line' with words and location and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "class" then, 'read a class line' with words and location and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "type" then, 'read a type line' with words and location and diagnostics, Return.
    If keyword is "relocation" then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not apply relocations yet, so every address in the image must already be final", Return.
    If keyword is "interpreter" or keyword is "needed" then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not link dynamically yet; it writes static executables, with no interpreter and no dynamic section", Return.
    'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{keyword}' is not a manifest statement".

(A line with the wrong number of words is reported with the shape it should
 have.)
To 'report the shape it should have' with a text called shape and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: this line should read: {shape}".

(section NAME KIND, then pairs, each said once: size BYTES, alignment
 BYTES, address WHERE (the address it was assembled for) and offset WHERE
 (where its bytes start in the image). file-offset is what a layout writes
 for the assembler, and is read and set aside here: voxelf works file
 offsets out for itself.)
To 'read a section line' with a list called words and a text called location and a list called sections and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'word count' is words's length.
    If 'word count' is less than 3 then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: a section line names the section and then its kind", Return.
    a text called 'section name' is element 2 of words.
    a text called kind is element 3 of words.
    a map called section is {"name": 'section name', "kind": kind, "size": -1, "alignment": 1}.
    a list called 'keys said' is [].
    a number called 'pair start' is 4.
    While 'pair start' is less than or equal to 'word count',
        a text called key is element 'pair start' of words,
        if 'pair start' is 'word count' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{key}' has no value after it", Return.
        a text called 'number text' is element {'pair start' add 1} of words,
        if not 'is one of' with key and ["size", "alignment", "address", "offset", "file-offset"] then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{key}' is not something a section line says".
        if 'is one of' with key and 'keys said' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{key}' is said twice; a section line says each thing once".
        append key to 'keys said',
        if key is "size" then, Set section's "size" to 'the number in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
        if key is "alignment" then, Set section's "alignment" to 'the number in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
        if key is "address" then, Set section's "assembled address" to 'the address in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
        if key is "offset" then, Set section's "image offset" to 'the number in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
        (A file offset is voxelf's own to work out, so it is only read, to be
         sure it is a number)
        if key is "file-offset" then, a number called 'file offset given' is 'the number in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
        Set 'pair start' to 'pair start' add 2.
    append section to sections.

(A symbol has one address, so a second line for the same name is refused
 rather than taken in place of the first.)
To 'read a symbol line' with a list called words and a text called location and a map called symbols and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 3 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "symbol NAME ADDRESS" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called 'symbol name' is element 2 of words.
    a text called 'number text' is element 3 of words.
    a number called address is 'the address in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.
    a list called 'names so far' is symbols's keys.
    If 'is one of' with 'symbol name' and 'names so far' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: the symbol {symbol name} is given a second time; a symbol has one address", Return.
    Set symbols's "{symbol name}" to address.

(Whether a path read from the manifest's directory climbs out of it: any
 part of it between slashes is "..". A path from the root is not read from
 that directory, so it is taken as it is written. The two periods after the
 Otherwise, which close it and the loop, need the Otherwise-period fix
 (#160), unreleased as of 0.4.16; see docs/bugs/unreleased.md.)
To 'climbs out of its directory' with a text called path.
    a buffer called spelling is "{path}".
    If byte 1 of spelling is '/' then, Return a boolean, false.
    Create a buffer called part.
    For each position from 1 to spelling's size,
        a number called 'the byte' is byte position of spelling,
        if 'the byte' is '/' then,
            if "{part}" is ".." then, Return a boolean, true.
            clear part.
        Otherwise,
            'append the byte' with 'the byte' and part..
    Return a boolean, "{part}" is "..".

(One image, because voxelf links one so far. A second one is refused by
 name rather than silently replacing the first. An image named from the
 manifest's directory stays inside it, so a manifest and its image move
 together and a manifest never reaches past the directory it stands in.)
To 'read an image line' with a list called words and a text called location and a map called settings and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 2 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "image PATH" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called 'image path' is element 2 of words.
    If 'climbs out of its directory' with 'image path' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: the image {image path} climbs out of the manifest's directory with '..'; name an image inside that directory, or give its whole path from /", Return.
    a text called 'image so far' is settings's "image".
    a text called 'where it was named' is settings's "image location".
    If 'image so far' is not empty then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not link several images together yet; it links one, and {where it was named} already named {image so far}", Return.
    Set settings's "image" to 'image path'.
    Set settings's "image location" to location.

To 'read a base line' with a list called words and a text called location and a map called settings and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 2 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "base ADDRESS" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called 'where it was given' is settings's "base location".
    If 'where it was given' is not empty then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: the load base is given a second time; {where it was given} already gave it", Return.
    Set settings's "base location" to location.
    a text called 'number text' is element 2 of words.
    Set settings's "base" to 'the address in' with 'number text' and location and diagnostics.

To 'read a machine line' with a list called words and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 2 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "machine x86-64" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called machine is element 2 of words.
    If not 'is one of' with machine and ["x86-64", "x86_64"] then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not write executables for {machine} yet, only for x86-64".

To 'read a class line' with a list called words and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 2 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "class 64" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called 'bits wide' is element 2 of words.
    If 'bits wide' is not "64" then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not write {bits wide}-bit ELF files yet, only 64-bit ones".

To 'read a type line' with a list called words and a text called location and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If words's length is not 2 then, 'report the shape it should have' with "type executable" and location and diagnostics, Return.
    a text called 'file type' is element 2 of words.
    If 'file type' is "executable" then, Return.
    If 'file type' is "shared" then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: voxelf does not write shared objects yet, only static executables", Return.
    'report' with diagnostics and "{location}: '{file type}' is not a type of file voxelf writes; the only one is executable".

(--- Linking --------------------------------------------------------------)

(Reads the manifest at 'manifest path' into settings, sections and symbols.
 An ELF file is refused instead, with its reason.)
To 'read the manifest at' with a text called 'manifest path' and a map called settings and a list called sections and a map called symbols and a buffer called diagnostics.
    If 'manifest path' is not available then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{manifest path}: there is no such file", Return.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    a buffer called content is 'the contents of' with 'manifest path' and 'the largest manifest' and diagnostics.
    If {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} is greater than 'errors before' then, Return.
    If 'is an elf file' with content then, 'refuse the elf file' with content and 'manifest path' and diagnostics, Return.
    'read the manifest' with content and 'manifest path' and settings and sections and symbols and diagnostics.

(The work of a link, which stops at the first stage that reports an error
 and leaves the counting to its caller.)
To 'link the manifest' with a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path' and a text called 'entry name' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    a map called settings is {"base": 0x400000, "base location": "", "image": "", "image location": ""}.
    a list called sections is [].
    a map called symbols is {}.
    'read the manifest at' with 'manifest path' and settings and sections and symbols and diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors found' is {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    a text called 'image name' is settings's "image".
    If 'image name' is empty then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{manifest path}: names no image, so there are no bytes to link", Return.
    a text called 'image path' is 'the path beside' with 'manifest path' and 'image name'.
    If 'image path' is not available then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{manifest path}: the image {image path} does not exist", Return.
    a buffer called image is 'the contents of' with 'image path' and 'the largest image' and diagnostics.
    Set 'errors found' to {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    a list called 'symbol names' is symbols's keys.
    If not 'is one of' with 'entry name' and 'symbol names' then, 'report' with diagnostics and "{manifest path}: there is no symbol called {entry name}, so the program has no entry point", Return.
    a number called 'entry address' is symbols's "{entry name}".
    a number called 'load base' is settings's "base".
    'write the executable' with sections and image and 'entry address' and 'load base' and 'output path' and diagnostics.

(The manifest written back with every section placed, for an assembler to
 assemble the image against: the load base, then one line for each section
 in layout order with its address and file offset. Like 'link the
 manifest', it leaves the counting to its caller.)
To 'lay out the manifest' with a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    a map called settings is {"base": 0x400000, "base location": "", "image": "", "image location": ""}.
    a list called sections is [].
    a map called symbols is {}.
    'read the manifest at' with 'manifest path' and settings and sections and symbols and diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors found' is {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    a number called 'load base' is settings's "base".
    a list called placed is 'place the sections' with sections and 'load base' and diagnostics.
    Set 'errors found' to {'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics} subtract 'errors before'.
    If 'errors found' is greater than 0 then, Return.
    Create a buffer called 'placed manifest'.
    append "base {load base:x}\n" to 'placed manifest'.
    (By position: a For each variable cannot be read as a map, LANGUAGE.md, Maps)
    For each position from 1 to placed's length,
        a map called section is element position of placed,
        a text called 'section name' is section's "name",
        a text called kind is section's "kind",
        a number called 'section size' is section's "size",
        a number called alignment is section's "alignment",
        a number called address is section's "address",
        a number called 'file offset' is section's "file offset",
        append "section {section name} {kind} size {section size} alignment {alignment} address {address:x} file-offset {file offset:x}\n" to 'placed manifest'.
    'write the bytes' with 'placed manifest' and 'output path' and diagnostics.

(--- The surface ----------------------------------------------------------)

(Links the manifest at 'manifest path' into a static executable at 'output
 path' that starts at the symbol 'entry name'. Answers 0, or how many errors
 there were, each one a line appended to diagnostics.)
To 'link and count the errors' with a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path' and a text called 'entry name' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    'link the manifest' with 'manifest path' and 'output path' and 'entry name' and diagnostics.
    Return a number, 'the errors since' with 'errors before' and diagnostics.

(Places the sections the manifest at 'manifest path' lists and writes the
 placed manifest to 'output path'. Answers as 'link and count the errors'
 does.)
To 'lay out and count the errors' with a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path' and a buffer called diagnostics.
    a number called 'errors before' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.
    'lay out the manifest' with 'manifest path' and 'output path' and diagnostics.
    Return a number, 'the errors since' with 'errors before' and diagnostics.

(The diagnostics of a failed call from a host that is not Vox are kept in a
 file beside the output, because a library has no storage of its own that
 lasts from one call to the next: a shared library may declare nothing at
 its top level but functions. 'linker diagnostics' reads that file and
 removes it.)
To 'the diagnostics path for' with a text called 'output path'.
    a text called 'diagnostics path' is "{output path}.diagnostics".
    Return a text, 'diagnostics path'.

To 'keep the diagnostics' with a buffer called diagnostics and a text called 'output path'.
    a text called 'diagnostics path' is 'the diagnostics path for' with 'output path'.
    If 'diagnostics path' is available then, Delete the file 'diagnostics path'.
    If diagnostics is empty then, Return.
    Create a buffer called 'complaints about the diagnostics'.
    'write the bytes' with diagnostics and 'diagnostics path' and 'complaints about the diagnostics'.

(For a host that is not Vox: a link, with the diagnostics kept for 'linker
 diagnostics'. Answers 0, or how many errors there were. The error flag is
 left alone: a shared library has its own, which the host cannot see.)
To 'link and keep the diagnostics' of a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path' and a text called 'entry name'.
    Create a buffer called diagnostics.
    'link the manifest' with 'manifest path' and 'output path' and 'entry name' and diagnostics.
    'keep the diagnostics' with diagnostics and 'output path'.
    Return a number, 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.

(For a host that is not Vox: a layout, with the diagnostics kept for
 'linker diagnostics'. Answers as 'link and keep the diagnostics' does.)
To 'lay out and keep the diagnostics' of a text called 'manifest path' and a text called 'output path'.
    Create a buffer called diagnostics.
    'lay out the manifest' with 'manifest path' and 'output path' and diagnostics.
    'keep the diagnostics' with diagnostics and 'output path'.
    Return a number, 'the number of diagnostics in' with diagnostics.

(What went wrong in the last call that wrote to 'output path', one error to
 a line; the empty text when nothing did. Reading the diagnostics removes
 them.)
To 'linker diagnostics' of a text called 'output path'.
    a text called 'diagnostics path' is 'the diagnostics path for' with 'output path'.
    If 'diagnostics path' is not available then, Return a text, "".
    Create a buffer called 'complaints about the diagnostics'.
    a buffer called diagnostics is 'the contents of' with 'diagnostics path' and 'the largest manifest' and 'complaints about the diagnostics'.
    Delete the file 'diagnostics path'.
    a text called 'what went wrong' is "{diagnostics}".
    Return a text, 'what went wrong'.
